    <color name="termux_on_accent_container">#0B1F44</color>
    <color name="termux_error">#BA1A1A</color>
    <!-- A window chip's "finished" dot. Material has no success role, and the busy accent
         cannot be it: done and running must not be the same colour. -->
    <color name="termux_chip_done">#1B6B3A</color>
    <!--
      The Display place's accent: warm, where the theme's roles are cool. Now authored the way
      every other role in this file is — an on-light ink here, its on-dark twin in values-night —
      so the place keeps one identity across the two modes instead of one colour. It used to be
      the pale peach in both, which reads 1.46:1 on termux_surface_panel_high: invisible on the
      light panels the display touchpad and the help overlay stand on. Same HCT hue as the night
      peach (55°) with a little more chroma, at the tone a light scheme's accent takes, so it is
      the same warm colour and it clears body text on those panels.
    -->
    <color name="termux_place_display">#8D4E19</color>
    <color name="termux_error_container">#FFDAD6</color>
    <color name="termux_on_error_container">#410002</color>
    <!--
      A scrollbar thumb is drawn over the terminal, and the terminal's background comes from the
      colour scheme rather than from the theme: a light scheme in dark mode is an ordinary choice.
      So this cannot be a mode-qualified answer. 40% white was invisible on any light background
      (1.03 against the light surface); a mid grey at 70% separates by 2.3 there and 2.9 on the
      dark one, which is the best single answer a mode-agnostic thumb can have.
    -->
    <color name="termux_scrollbar">#B3808080</color>

    <!--
      Overridden at runtime by LauncherSchemeTheme when the chrome follows the terminal colour
      scheme. Deliberately declared in values/ only: a ResourcesLoader entry carries no config
      qualifiers, so a values-night twin of this colour would outrank the override at night — which
      is also why its static fallback is the same mode-agnostic grey as termux_scrollbar rather than
      white light for one mode and nothing for the other.
    -->
    <color name="launcher_scheme_scrollbar">#B3808080</color>

